Article 25-3 (Request for provision of financial information) (1) The specific details of data or information under Article 34(1)1 and 2 of the Act shall be as follows:
  1. Financial information under Article 34(1)1 of the Act: The following data or information:
  a. Demand deposits such as ordinary deposits, savings deposits, and free savings deposits: The average balance within the recent three months;
  b. Savings deposits, such as term deposits, installment deposits, and term savings: The account balance and the total amount deposited;
  c. Stocks, beneficiary certificates, investments, equity shares: The final market value; in such cases, the value of unlisted stocks shall be assessed under Article 54(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Inheritance Tax and Gift Tax Act;
  d. Receivables, notes, checks, debt certificates, and certificates of preemptive right to new stocks: The face value;
  e. Annuity savings: The amount paid regularly or the final balance;
  2. Credit information under Article 34(1)2 of the Act: The following data or information:
  a. The current status of loans and the details of arrears;
  b. Outstanding credit card balance.
  (2) "Insurance-related data or information prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 34(1)3 of the Act means the following data or information:
  1. Insurance policy: The amount to be refunded where an insurance contract is canceled or insurance money paid within the recent one year;
  2. Annuity insurance: The amount to be refunded where an insurance contract is canceled or the amount paid regularly.
  (3) Where a person who applies for a loan related to the Fund or his or her spouse submits a written consent under Article 34(2) of the Act, he or she may submit a written consent to provision of financial information, etc. prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to the Minister of the Land, Infrastructure and Transport in person, by mail or fax, or through an information and communications network. <Amended on Apr. 9, 2024>
  (4) HUG may request a person who applies for a guarantee for return of the deposit for leasing housing on a deposit basis pursuant to Article 34(3) of the Act to submit a written consent necessary for obtaining information concerning the establishment of a security right of a financial institution, such as a pledge on a guarantee for return of the deposit for leasing housing on a deposit basis, and the amount of the security right established. <Added on Apr. 9, 2024>
  (5) Where a person who applies for a guarantee for return of the deposit for leasing housing on a deposit basis submits a written consent pursuant to Article 34(3) of the Act, he or she may directly submit a written consent to provision of financial information, etc., as determined by HUG, to HUG, or by mail or fax, or through an information and communications network. <Added on Apr. 9, 2024>
  [This Article Added on Jul. 23, 2019]

Article 25-4 (Provision of financial information) (1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (including the Minister of Health and Welfare entrusted with duties pursuant to Article 34-4 of the Act; hereafter in this Article, the same shall apply) and HUG request the head of a financial institution or the head of the Committee for Centralized Management of Credit Information under Article 25(2)1 of the Credit Information Use and Protection Act (hereinafter referred to as "credit information collection agency") to provide financial information, credit information, or insurance information under Article 34(1)1 through 3 of the Act and Article 25-3(4) of this Decree (hereinafter referred to as "financial information, etc.") on a person who applies for a loan related to the Fund under Article 25-3(3), his or her spouse, or a person who applies for a guarantee for return of the deposit for leasing housing on a deposit basis under Article 25-3(4) (hereinafter referred to as "applicant, etc.") pursuant to Article 34-2(1) and (2) of the Act, he, she, or it shall include the following matters in the details of such request: <Amended on Aug. 4, 2022; Apr. 9, 2024>
  1. Names and resident registration numbers of the applicant, etc.;
  2. Scope of financial information, etc. requested to be provided, and the base date and period of inquiring such financial information, etc.;
  3. Legal grounds for the request;
  4. Purposes of use;
  5. Personal information on the person in charge or the responsible person, such as his or her name and duties.
  (2) Where the head of a financial institution or the head of a credit information collection agency requested to provide financial information, etc. under Article 34-2(1) and (2) of the Act provides such financial information, etc. to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport and HUG, he or she shall include the following matters in the details thereof: <Amended on Apr. 9, 2024>
  1. Names and resident registration numbers of the applicant, etc.;
  2. Name of the financial institution or the credit information collection agency which provides the financial information, etc.;
  3. Names of financial products on which the financial information, etc. are to be provided and account numbers thereof;
  4. Details of the financial information, etc.
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport and HUG may request the head of a financial institution or the head of a credit information collection agency to provide financial information, etc. using the information and communications network of an association, a federation, or a national federation joined by such financial institution or credit information collection agency. <Amended on Apr. 9, 2024>
  [This Article Added on Jul. 23, 2019]

Article 26 (Management of personally identifiable information) (1) If it is inevitable for performing any of the following affairs, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (including persons entrusted or re-entrusted with the duties of the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport pursuant to Article 10(2) or (3) of the Act) may manage data which includes resident registration numbers, passport numbers, or alien registration numbers referred to in Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act: <Amended on Nov. 1, 2016; Jul. 23, 2019>
  1. Affairs relating to the purchase of national housing bonds prescribed in Article 8 of the Act;
  2. Affairs relating to loans referred to in Article 9(1)1a through e and g of the Act (including information on lessors and household members);
  3. Affairs relating to financing, investments, or loans referred to in Article 9(2)2 of the Act;
  4. Affairs relating to savings for housing under Article 56(2) of the Housing Act among the affairs relating to the operation and management of the Fund under Article 10 of the Act.
  (2) If it is inevitable for performing any of the following affairs, HUG may manage data which includes resident registration numbers referred to in subparagraph 1 of Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act, passport numbers referred to in subparagraph 2 of Article 19 of that Decree, or foreigner registration numbers referred to subparagraph 4 of Article 19 of that Decree:
  1. Affairs relating to guarantees prescribed in Article 26(1)2 of the Act;
  2. Affairs relating to the execution of guarantees as well as the exercise of the right to indemnity prescribed in Article 26(1)3 of the Act;
  3. Affairs relating to guarantees for asset-backed securities prescribed in Article 26(1)4 of the Act as well as the management of securitization assets prescribed in Article 26(1)5 of the Act;
  4. Affairs relating to acquisition, management, and renovation of real estate as well as receipt of requests for disposal thereof under Article 26(1)6 of the Act;
  5. Affairs relating to guarantees, provision of guarantees, or loans (including affairs relating to the filing of claims following business operations on guarantees, provision of guarantees, or loans), among the affairs entrusted pursuant to Article 26(1)7 of the Act;
  6. Affairs relating to underwriting of a trust prescribed in Article 26(2) of the Act;
  7. Affairs relating to the affairs referred to in Article 22(1)1 of the Act.

Article 27 (Disclosure of names of habitual defaulters) (1) "Reason prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as that the lessor dies or civil litigation is pending with respect to the liability for reimbursement" in the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 34-5(1) of the Act means any of the following:
  1. Where the lessor under the main sentence, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 34-5(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "habitual defaulter") dies or is judicially declared disappeared pursuant to Article 27 of the Civil Act;
  2. Where civil litigation is pending with respect to the liability for reimbursement (hereinafter referred to as "liability for reimbursement") under Article 34-5(1) of the Act (excluding where civil litigation is pending with regard to part of multiple liabilities for reimbursement and the requirements for disclosure under Article 34-5(1) of the Act are satisfied only with the remaining liabilities for reimbursement);
  3. Where the habitual defaulter fully fulfills the liability for reimbursement;
  4. Where the Deliberation Committee on Disclosure of Lessors' Information under Article 34-5(4) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Information Disclosure Deliberation Committee") deems it necessary to exclude the habitual defaulter from those subject to disclosure as he or she fulfills at least 50/100 of the liability for reimbursement and provides a sufficient explanation of a plan for fulfilling and financing the remaining liability;
  5. Cases equivalent to those specified in subparagraphs 1 through 4 where the Information Disclosure Deliberation Committee deems that disclosing the name, etc. of the habitual defaulter brings no practical benefits or that such disclosure is inappropriate.
  (2) "Guarantee liability prescribed by Presidential Decree ... including a guarantee for rental deposits" in Article 34-5(1)1 of the Act means any of the following: <Amended on Apr. 9, 2024>
  1. Guarantee for rental deposits under Article 21(1)2;
  2. Guarantee for return of the deposit for leasing housing on a deposit basis under Article 21(1)3-2.
  (3) Where HUG intends to notify a person subject to disclosure of the names, etc. of habitual defaulters of the fact that the person is subject to such disclosure pursuant to Article 34-5(3) of the Act, it shall urge him or her to fulfill the liability for reimbursement and inform him or her to submit explanatory materials within two months from the date of such notification.
  (4) Where a person subject to disclosure of the names, etc. of habitual defaulters or his or her heir or legal representative provides an explanation of a reason referred to in any of paragraph (1)1 through 4, HUG shall delete the name, etc. of the person disclosed following deliberation and resolution by the Information Disclosure Deliberation Committee.
  (5) Where HUG discloses names, etc. pursuant to Article 34-5(1) of the Act or deletes the names, etc. disclosed pursuant to paragraph (4) of this Article, it shall inform the person subject to disclosure or his or her heir or legal representative of such fact without delay.
  (6) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (5), matters necessary for the methods, procedures, etc. for disclosing the names, etc. of habitual defaulters shall be determined by HUG.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 19, 2023]

Article 28 (Composition and operation of of Deliberation Committee on Disclosure of Lessors' Information) (1) An Information Disclosure Deliberation Committee shall be established under HUG to deliberate on whether to disclose the names, etc. of habitual defaulters under Article 34-5(1) of the Act.
  (2) The Information Disclosure Deliberation Committee shall be composed of 11 members, including one chairperson.
  (3) The chairperson of the Information Disclosure Deliberation Committee shall be appointed by the President of HUG from among its members, and members shall be appointed or commissioned by the President of HUG from among the following persons:
  1. Three executive officers and employees of HUG;
  2. Three persons who served or have been serving as a judge, prosecutor, or attorney-at-law for at least five years;
  3. Three persons who have held or hold the position of associate professor or higher in a department related to real estate, finance, or law in a university or college under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act;
  4. Two persons with extensive knowledge of and experience in real estate, finance, or law.
  (4) The term of office of a member under paragraph (3)2 through 4 shall be two years.
  (5) The chairperson shall represent the Information Disclosure Deliberation Committee and exercise general supervision over its duties; provided, where the chairperson is unable to perform his or her duties due to any unavoidable reason, a member designated in advance by the chairperson shall act on behalf of the chairperson.
  (6) A majority of the members of the Information Disclosure Deliberation Committee shall constitute a quorum, and any resolution thereof shall require the concurring vote of a majority of those present.
  (7) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (6), matters necessary for the composition, operation, etc. of the Information Disclosure Deliberation Committee shall be determined by HUG.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 19, 2023]

Article 29 (Exclusion of, challenge to, or recusal of, members) (1) A member of the Information Disclosure Deliberation Committee shall be excluded from its deliberation and resolution in any of the following cases:
  1. Where the member or the current or former spouse of the member becomes a party to the relevant agenda item (where a party is a corporation, an organization, etc., the executive officers thereof shall be included; hereafter in this subparagraph and subparagraph 2, the same shall apply) or is a joint right holder or a joint obligor with a party to the relevant agenda item;
  2. Where the member is or was a relative of a party to the relevant agenda item;
  3. Where the member has given testimony, presented a statement, rendered advice, conducted research, provided services, or made an appraisal with respect to the relevant agenda item;
  4. Where the member or the corporation, organization, etc. to which the member belongs is or was an agent of a party to the relevant agenda item.
  (2) Where a member is subject to exclusion on any ground referred to in paragraph (1) or the circumstances indicate that it would be impracticable to expect fair deliberations and resolutions by a member, any party may file a motion for challenge to the member with the Information Disclosure Deliberation Committee, which shall decide whether or not to accept the motion by its resolution. In such cases, the member to whom the motion for challenge is filed shall not participate in such resolution.
  (3) Where a member is subject to exclusion or challenge on any ground specified in paragraph (1) or (2), he or she shall recuse himself or herself from deliberation and resolution on the relevant agenda item.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 19, 2023]

Article 30 (Dismissal of members) Where a member under Article 28(3)2 through 4 falls under any of the following cases, the President of HUG may dismiss the member:
  1. Where the member is no longer able to perform his or her duties due to mental or physical weakness;
  2. Where the member engages in any misconduct in connection with his or her duties;
  3. Where the member is deemed unsuitable as a member due to neglect of duty, injury to dignity, or any other reason;
  4. Where the member fails to recuse himself or herself although he or she falls under Article 29(1) or (2);
  5. Where the member voluntarily expresses his or her intention that it is impracticable for him or her to perform his or her duties.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 19, 2023]

